{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-54488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-54488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/39723169158aa3bf15f57244e3ac4c4a3ffb1c2b0e3fc41b6db712cfab8ddbd9.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases.  Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-54488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "29aa2b969b1ad5b819af0054768d921cde6337a62f1a5a8972adac830fe8b4c4"} {"question" "q-54488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-54488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-54488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x})=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x}) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({7}^{-1})}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/f2858b7d6adc07c0f2196bbe8170f17fa02aff19d494726c08f7c03a419afd53.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function approaches zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "29aa2b969b1ad5b819af0054768d921cde6337a62f1a5a8972adac830fe8b4c4"} {"question" "q-339080" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339080" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/99c2329c99005af33f4b62d61547e18dcaa7f04afc931df57439bd1ca08ff35c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "29aa2b969b1ad5b819af0054768d921cde6337a62f1a5a8972adac830fe8b4c4"} {"question" "q-339407" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339407" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2\\cdot {e}^{4x-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2\\cdot {e}^{4x-3} & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{4x-3} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{-3}\\cdot {e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{-3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={({e}^{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/6590b80777c49b3d018fa38ce79c7fb14103365f783dac18135b8d3637ee724d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe negative factor $-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot \\infty =-\\infty .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "29aa2b969b1ad5b819af0054768d921cde6337a62f1a5a8972adac830fe8b4c4"} {"question" "q-339411" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339411" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\infty"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({5}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{-2x}) \\\\  & ={5}^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{-2x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({5}^{-2})}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{{5}^{2}})}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/3795890dac6f542c8aa75d61deb8a03192a41532270b74eafcd972e82ac6721e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x}=125\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "29aa2b969b1ad5b819af0054768d921cde6337a62f1a5a8972adac830fe8b4c4"} {"question" "q-339413" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"} {"question" "q-339413" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d1b2f4ff63dde640de491aec6f8ecd3f2884d714613f8b9263c8a71ef97478"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1180 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
